  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is infrasound?",
      "a": "Infrasound is sound with a pitch below about 20 hertz, too low for human ears. Elephants, whales, volcanoes and storms all make it, and it can travel very long distances.",
      "asks": [
        "What does infrasonic mean?",
        "Can people hear infrasound?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can elephants hear through their feet?",
      "a": "Yes, in a way. Elephants have sensitive nerve endings in their feet and trunk that can feel ground vibrations from distant rumbles. Scientists have seen elephants lean forward and lift a foot as if listening.",
      "asks": [
        "Do elephants feel vibrations?",
        "How do elephants sense the ground?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How far can elephant rumbles travel?",
      "a": "Through the air, low rumbles can usually be heard by other elephants a few kilometres away, and up to around 10 kilometres on calm evenings. Ground vibrations may carry similar distances.",
      "asks": [
        "How do elephants talk over long distances?",
        "Can elephants hear each other miles away?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do low sounds travel so far?",
      "a": "Air absorbs low-pitched sound much less than high-pitched sound, and long low waves bend around obstacles like trees and hills. So low rumbles fade slowly.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do deep sounds carry?",
        "Why can I hear bass from far away?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do elephants say with rumbles?",
      "a": "Rumbles help elephants find each other, coordinate moving as a herd, warn of danger and find mates. Each elephant's rumble is a little different, so they can recognise who is calling.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do elephants rumble?",
        "Can elephants recognise voices?"
      ]
    }
  ],
